<p>Water is coming through the ceiling after a storm, or a kitchen fire has left smoke in every room, and the insurer has given you a claim number. Somewhere in that first week, most San Marcos property owners ask the same question: <strong>do I need a public adjuster</strong>, or can I handle this claim myself? The honest answer is that it depends on the size of the loss, how clear the coverage is, how the insurer is responding, and how much time you can give the process. This guide gives you a way to decide, with a simple decision table, the signs that point toward hiring help, the situations where you probably do not need it, and what to look for if you choose a public adjuster in San Marcos.</p>

<p>It is general information, not legal advice, written by a licensed Texas public adjuster. Every policy and every loss is different.</p>

<h2>What a public adjuster is, in plain English</h2>

<p>A public adjuster is an insurance adjuster you hire. In Texas, public adjusters are licensed and regulated by the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) under Insurance Code Chapter 4102, and every public adjuster contract must state “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY.” The adjuster your insurer assigns, whether a staff employee or an independent adjuster hired after a big storm, evaluates the claim for the insurance company.</p>

<p>On a typical property claim, a public adjuster will:</p>
<ul>
<li>Read the policy, including endorsements, deductibles, limits, and exclusions.</li>
<li>Inspect and document the damage to the building, other structures, and contents.</li>
<li>Prepare a detailed, line-item estimate of what it will cost to repair or replace.</li>
<li>Present the claim to the insurer, handle requests for information, and meet the insurer’s adjuster on re-inspections.</li>
<li>Track deadlines and follow up until the claim is resolved.</li>
</ul>

<p>A public adjuster is not a contractor and cannot do the repairs on a claim they adjust, and is not a lawyer and cannot give legal advice. For a deeper explanation, see <a href="/what-is-a-public-adjuster-and-how-to-hire-one/">what a public adjuster is and how to hire one</a> or <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster</a>.</p>

<h2>Do I need a public adjuster? A quick decision table</h2>

<p>Find the row that best describes your situation. This is a starting point, not a rule.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Probably handle it yourself</th><th>Consider a public adjuster</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Size of the loss</td><td>Small repair, close to or below the deductible</td><td>Roof replacement, major water, fire, or flood damage; contents and living expenses involved</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Clearly covered, simple policy</td><td>Exclusions in play (wear and tear, mold, gradual leaks), ACV roof terms, ordinance or law questions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insurer’s response</td><td>Prompt inspection, clear estimate, reasonable answers</td><td>Slow responses, repeated document requests, low estimate, or denial</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimate vs. contractor bid</td><td>Close to each other</td><td>Large gap, or the estimate leaves out items your contractor says are needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Damage types</td><td>One room or one component</td><td>Several types at once (roof plus interior water, fire plus smoke plus water)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Property type</td><td>Single-family home, simple claim</td><td>Commercial property, rental or multifamily, business income losses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Your time and comfort</td><td>You have time and are comfortable reading the policy and negotiating</td><td>You are displaced, working full time, or overwhelmed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>If most of your answers land in the right-hand column, it is worth at least getting a second opinion. If they land in the middle column, you may be well served handling the claim yourself, with careful documentation.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/7-Signs-You-Should-Hire-a-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Couple reviewing insurance paperwork at a table with a public adjuster" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Reviewing the policy and the insurer’s estimate side by side is often the first step in deciding whether you need help.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Seven signs you should consider hiring a public adjuster</h2>

<h3>1. The loss is large</h3>
<p>A full roof replacement, a kitchen fire, a burst pipe that soaked several rooms, or flooding that reached drywall and flooring all involve many line items and several parts of the policy. The more items there are, the more room there is for something to be missed or undervalued.</p>

<h3>2. The insurer’s estimate does not match what your contractor sees</h3>
<p>Compare the insurer’s estimate with a contractor’s bid, line by line. Missing tear-out, missing trim and paint, missing roof components such as drip edge and flashing, or unit prices that look low are common gaps. TDI’s own guidance suggests sharing the insurer’s estimate with your contractor and talking to the insurer about differences. If that conversation goes nowhere, a professional estimate review may help. See <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</p>

<h3>3. The claim was denied, or partly denied</h3>
<p>Denials are often based on a cause-of-loss conclusion: wear and tear, long-term leakage, pre-existing damage, or an exclusion. Sometimes that conclusion is right. Sometimes it rests on an incomplete inspection. A public adjuster can review the denial letter and the evidence and tell you whether there is a documented basis to ask for reconsideration. Our guide on <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-claim-was-denied-in-san-marcos/">what to do if your claim was denied in San Marcos</a> covers the first steps.</p>

<h3>4. The claim is stalled</h3>
<p>Texas’s Prompt Payment of Claims law (Insurance Code Chapter 542) sets deadlines for insurers to acknowledge a claim, request information, and accept or reject it, with extensions available after weather catastrophes. If weeks pass without a decision or you keep getting new document requests, it may be time for someone to organize the file and track those timelines. Read <a href="/why-property-insurance-claims-are-delayed-texas/">why property insurance claims are delayed in Texas</a>.</p>

<h3>5. There are several kinds of damage at once</h3>
<p>Storm claims in Hays County often combine roof damage with interior water damage. Fire claims combine burned materials, smoke, soot, and water from firefighting. Each damage type has its own documentation needs and sometimes its own coverage limits.</p>

<h3>6. The policy language is unclear to you</h3>
<p>Replacement cost vs. actual cash value, recoverable depreciation, percentage wind and hail deductibles, roof payment schedules, ordinance or law coverage, and additional living expenses all affect the check you receive. If you cannot tell from your declarations page how your loss will be paid, a policy review may be worthwhile.</p>

<h3>7. You do not have the time or capacity</h3>
<p>A large claim can mean dozens of photos, inventories, re-inspections, emails, and phone calls over weeks or months. If you are displaced, running a business, or caring for family, delegating the claim work can be a reasonable choice.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>When you probably do not need a public adjuster</h2>

<p>A balanced answer to “do I need a public adjuster” has to include the times when the answer is no. You may be better off handling the claim yourself when:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The damage is minor.</strong> If the repair cost is near your deductible, a fee on a small payment rarely makes sense.</li>
<li><strong>The insurer is responsive and the numbers line up.</strong> If the estimate is close to a reputable contractor’s bid and covers all the damaged areas, there may be little to add.</li>
<li><strong>Coverage is straightforward.</strong> A clearly covered event with no exclusions in play and no disputes about cause.</li>
<li><strong>You are comfortable doing the work.</strong> Some owners have the time and experience to document, read the policy, and negotiate on their own.</li>
<li><strong>The issue is legal, not adjusting.</strong> If you are already in litigation or the dispute is about coverage law or bad faith, a Texas attorney is the right professional.</li>
</ul>

<p>Even then, document everything: photos before cleanup, receipts for emergency repairs, written notes of every call, and copies of every estimate. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lays it out.</p>

<h2>What a public adjuster costs, and how to think about value</h2>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. A few details from TDI are worth knowing before you sign:</p>
<ul>
<li>The fee can be based on the total settlement, not only on the amount in dispute.</li>
<li>You can ask for the fee to be written as a dollar amount instead of a percentage, and you can try to negotiate it.</li>
<li>You may still owe a fee if the insurer does not increase its offer, and you still pay your deductible.</li>
<li>You have 72 hours after signing to cancel the contract.</li>
<li>If the insurer pays or commits in writing to pay policy limits within 72 hours after the loss is reported, Texas law does not allow a percentage fee on that payment.</li>
</ul>

<p>The useful question is not “will a public adjuster get me more,” because no one can promise that. It is “is there a realistic gap between what the policy should pay for this damage and what I have been offered, and is my time worth spending to close it myself?” Our article <a href="/are-public-adjusters-worth-it/">are public adjusters worth it?</a> looks at that question in more detail.</p>

<h2>San Marcos risks that make claims more complicated</h2>

<p>Local conditions shape the claims we see. NOAA’s Storm Events Database lists 180 hail reports in Hays County since 1967, peaking in April and May, and 127 flash flood reports. San Marcos sits along the Balcones Escarpment in what is often called Flash Flood Alley, and the Blanco and San Marcos Rivers flooded severely in May 2015. Winter Storm Uri in February 2021 caused widespread burst pipes across the region. Each of these perils raises different coverage questions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hail and wind:</strong> roof age, wear-and-tear disputes, matching, and percentage deductibles. See <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Flood:</strong> standard homeowners policies generally exclude flood, so coverage comes from NFIP or private flood policies with their own rules.</li>
<li><strong>Freeze and water:</strong> sudden pipe bursts are often covered, while long-term leaks and resulting mold may be limited or excluded.</li>
<li><strong>Foundation and plumbing:</strong> slab homes on expansive clay soils east of I-35 can raise cause-of-loss questions when a plumbing leak and foundation movement appear together.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history page</a> has more on the local record.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/How-to-Choose-the-Right-Public-Adjuster-in-San-Marcos-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster holding a phone and clipboard next to a tarped, storm-damaged roof" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Choosing a public adjuster starts with verifying the license and reading the contract carefully.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How to choose a public adjuster in San Marcos</h2>

<p>If you decide to hire help, take a little time to compare San Marcos public adjusters before signing anything.</p>

<h3>What to check</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>License.</strong> Confirm an active Texas public adjuster license. The TDI Help Line (800-252-3439) can tell you whether a public adjuster is licensed, how many complaints have been filed, and whether TDI has taken disciplinary action.</li>
<li><strong>The contract.</strong> It should use TDI-approved terms, state the fee clearly, include the “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY” statement, and explain the 72-hour cancellation right. Ask whether they will inspect the property and talk to the insurer for you.</li>
<li><strong>Relevant experience.</strong> Ask about experience with your type of loss: roof, water, fire, flood, or commercial.</li>
<li><strong>Communication.</strong> You should know who is handling your file and how often you will hear from them.</li>
<li><strong>Local familiarity.</strong> Knowledge of Hays County construction, the City of San Marcos building codes, and local storm history can help when documenting a claim.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Red flags</h3>
<ul>
<li>Promises of a specific payout, a guaranteed new roof, or “you pay nothing” claims.</li>
<li>Door-to-door solicitation during a natural disaster or after 9 p.m., which Texas law prohibits for public adjusters.</li>
<li>Anyone offering to be both your public adjuster and your contractor on the same claim.</li>
<li>Pressure to sign the same day, or a contract with blanks.</li>
<li>Advice to stop talking to your insurer entirely, or legal advice from someone who is not a lawyer.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our article on <a href="/how-to-find-a-public-adjuster/">how to find a public adjuster</a> includes a list of questions to ask in the first call. If you are worried you waited too long, read <a href="/when-is-it-too-late-to-hire-a-public-adjuster/">when is it too late to hire a public adjuster</a>.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes when handling a claim alone</h2>
<ul>
<li>Throwing out damaged materials or contents before they are photographed and the insurer has had a chance to see them.</li>
<li>Accepting the first estimate without comparing it to a contractor’s bid.</li>
<li>Missing secondary damage: interior stains from a roof leak, damaged outbuildings and fences, or smoke in the HVAC system.</li>
<li>Not keeping receipts for emergency repairs, hotel stays, and meals while displaced.</li>
<li>Misunderstanding depreciation and not claiming recoverable depreciation after repairs are completed.</li>
<li>Letting policy deadlines pass.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>

<p>Do you need a public adjuster? For a small, clearly covered claim with a responsive insurer, probably not. For a large, complex, stalled, underpaid, or denied claim, or when you simply cannot carry the workload, professional help is worth considering. Either way, you should understand your policy and your options before you accept a settlement.</p>

